TEXT 96 -80 Left 3 ;#417 Current gain against frequency for the fT test circuit
TEXT 96 -16 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PRzytyqwK5U
TEXT 96 32 Left 2 ;Sweeping the same circuit draws the whole current-gain curve: flat at low frequency, then falling at 20 dB per decade, and crossing a gain of one at the transition frequency.
TEXT 96 600 Left 2 !.ac dec 200 10k 1G
TEXT 96 640 Left 2 !.meas ac BETA_LF FIND mag(I(Vcprobe)/I(Rb)) AT=10k
TEXT 96 680 Left 2 !.meas ac HFE_300K FIND mag(I(Vcprobe)/I(Rb)) AT=300k
TEXT 96 720 Left 2 !.meas ac HFE_3MEG FIND mag(I(Vcprobe)/I(Rb)) AT=3Meg
TEXT 96 760 Left 2 !.meas ac HFE_30MEG FIND mag(I(Vcprobe)/I(Rb)) AT=30Meg
TEXT 96 800 Left 2 !.meas ac FT WHEN mag(I(Vcprobe)/I(Rb))=1
TEXT 96 840 Left 2 !.save I(Vcprobe) I(Rb)
TEXT 96 904 Left 2 ;The same circuit at the same 10 mA bias, swept as a small-signal analysis. The plotted quantity is I(Vcprobe)/I(Rb), the collector current divided by the base current, which is the current gain.
TEXT 96 944 Left 2 ;The drive is 1 V because a ratio of two currents does not depend on the drive level. This is the curve Alan draws freehand on page 1 of his notes, computed for this transistor.
TEXT 96 984 Left 2 ;VCPROBE is a zero-volt source standing for the TCP0030 current probe on the collector lead, and I(Rb) is the base current his differential probe reads across the 560 ohm.
TEXT 96 1024 Left 2 ;Q1 is a 2N2222. The device in the video is identified only as an NPN silicon BJT with hFE = 198 on his DCA75, so this is a stand-in with a similar low-frequency gain.
